.* WRKXDBPRF - help panel group of the command and of the displays of XDBPRFM.
.* Source member WRKXDBPRFH in QXDBSRC; object WRKXDBPRF type *PNLGRP.
.* CRTPNLGRP PNLGRP(lib/WRKXDBPRF) SRCFILE(lib/QXDBSRC) SRCMBR(WRKXDBPRFH)
.* The command is created with HLPPNLGRP(lib/WRKXDBPRF) HLPID(*CMD).
.* Display file XDBPRFD names the modules below in its help specifications
.* (prf/gen_dds.py): one module for each field or part of a display, so that
.* F1 shows the help of the place the cursor is in.  The help of the command
.* imports all of them.  A module that is added or renamed here has to be
.* added or renamed in gen_dds.py too (build/chk_help.py compares the two).
.* In the text a colon is written &colon. and an ampersand &amp.
:PNLGRP.
.* ------------------------------------------------------------- command
:HELP NAME='WRKXDBPRF'.
Work with XDB Profiles - Help
:P.
The Work with XDB Profiles (WRKXDBPRF) command shows the connection
profiles of XDBARDDRV and lets you add, change, copy, delete and test
them.
:P.
XDBARDDRV lets SQL on this system work with databases that are not
Db2. After CONNECT TO with the name of a profile, the SQL statements
of interactive SQL or of a program run on a PostgreSQL, MySQL or
Microsoft SQL Server database. The driver is native ILE code and runs
inside the job that connects. XDBARDDRV is the name of the software;
its objects carry the short prefix XDB.
:P.
A profile belongs to one relational database (RDB) name. It tells
the driver where the database is, how to sign on, and how the data is
to be given to Db2. The profiles are kept in files XDBPRF and XDBPRO
and the passwords in validation list XDBVLDL. All three are in the
library of the driver, which is shown at the top of the list.
:P.
The command has no parameters. It shows the list of the profiles.
:P.
The command is also the installer. After the objects of XDBARDDRV were
restored into a library, F9 on the list shows the Setup display, which
checks the installation in that library and does what is still to do.
When the profile files are not there yet, the Setup display comes
first.
:P.
:HP2.Restrictions&colon.:EHP2.
:UL.
:LI.
To add, change or delete profiles you need *CHANGE authority to
files XDBPRF and XDBPRO.
:LI.
To store or remove a password you need *CHANGE authority to
validation list XDBVLDL.
:LI.
Passwords can only be stored when system value QRETSVRSEC (retain
server security data) is 1.
:LI.
Option 8 runs the Add RDB Directory Entry (ADDRDBDIRE) command and
needs the authority that command asks for.
:LI.
A change takes effect at the next CONNECT. A connection that is
open keeps the settings it started with.
:LI.
The Setup display can be looked at by everybody. Its steps for the
owner profile need *SECADM and *ALLOBJ special authority; the other
steps need the authority to change the objects of the library.
:EUL.
:P.
:HP2.Help on the displays&colon.:EHP2.
On every display of the command, F1 shows the help for the field or
the part of the display the cursor is in, and F2 in the help shows
the help of the whole display.
:P.
:HP2.Topics of this help&colon.:EHP2.
:UL COMPACT.
:LI.The list of profiles and its options
:LI.The fields of a profile
:LI.The password
:LI.The options of a profile and their keywords
:LI.The RDB directory entry
:LI.The connection test
:LI.Using a profile from SQL and from a program
:LI.Setup&colon. installing, moving and checking
:LI.When something does not work
:LI.Licence
:EUL.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/LIST'.
