:IMHELP NAME='XDB/SETUP'.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/OWN'.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/OPT'.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/LINES'.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/STS'.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/KEYS'.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/CMD'.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/HOW'.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/PROBLEMS'.
:IMHELP NAME='XDB/LICENCE'.
:EHELP.
.* -------------------------------------------------------- all displays
:HELP NAME='XDB/GENERAL'.
XDBARDDRV profiles - Help
:XH3.The displays of WRKXDBPRF
:P.
These displays keep the connection profiles of XDBARDDRV&colon. one
profile for each database that SQL on this system reaches with
CONNECT TO. They belong to program XDBPRFM, which is the program of
command WRKXDBPRF and also the installer (F9=Setup on the list of
profiles). The program works with the library it was started from;
the library is shown at the top of the list.
:P.
:HP2.How the help works&colon.:EHP2.
:UL COMPACT.
:LI.
F1 shows the help for the field, the column or the line of keys the
cursor is in.
:LI.
F2 in the help shows the help of the whole display. On the list of
profiles it also holds the topics about SQL, about problems and
about the licence.
:LI.
F1 on the command WRKXDBPRF, typed on a command line, shows all of
it in one piece.
:EUL.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------- list
:HELP NAME='XDB/LIST'.
The list of profiles - Help
:XH3.The list of profiles and its options
:P.
The list shows one line for each profile&colon. the RDB name, the
database type (PG, MYSQL or MSSQL), the host, the database and the
status (A active, D disabled).
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/LIST/POS'.
Position to - Help
:XH4.Position to
:P.
Type the first characters of an RDB name and press Enter. The list
starts at the first profile from that name on. Blank it out, or press
F5, to see all profiles again.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/LIST/OPT'.
Options of the list - Help
:XH4.Options of the list
:P.
Type an option next to one or more profiles and press Enter.
:PARML.
:PT.2=Change
:PD.
Change the fields of the profile. The RDB name cannot be changed;
use 3=Copy and then delete the old profile.
:PT.3=Copy
:PD.
Add a profile with the same values under another RDB name. The
options of the profile are copied too. The password is not copied;
set it with F10 on the new profile.
:PT.4=Delete
:PD.
Delete the profile after a confirmation. Its options and its stored
password are deleted with it. The RDB directory entry is not touched;
remove it with the Work with RDB Directory Entries (WRKRDBDIRE)
command when it is no longer wanted.
:PT.5=Display
:PD.
Show the profile without changing it. The password is never shown,
only whether one is stored.
:PT.7=Options
:PD.
Work with the options of the profile&colon. settings beyond the
fields of the profile, most of them particular to one database type.
:PT.8=RDB entry
:PD.
Add the RDB directory entry of the same name, which makes CONNECT TO
call the driver.
:PT.9=Test
:PD.
Connect to the database with the values of the profile and show the
result.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/LIST/KEYS'.
Function keys of the list - Help
:XH4.Function keys
:PARML.
:PT.F1=Help
:PD.
Show the help for the place the cursor is in. F2 in the help shows
the help of the whole display.
:PT.F3=Exit
:PD.End the command.
:PT.F5=Refresh
:PD.Read the profiles again and show all of them.
:PT.F6=Add
:PD.Add a profile.
:PT.F9=Setup
:PD.
Show the Setup display with the checks of the installation in this
library. A message on the list tells when the setup has something
to do.
:PT.F12=Cancel
:PD.Go back to the previous display.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ------------------------------------------------------------- profile
:HELP NAME='XDB/PROFILE'.
The fields of a profile - Help
:XH3.The fields of a profile
:P.
The display is used to add a profile (F6 on the list), to change or
copy one (options 2 and 3) and to show one (option 5, where nothing
can be typed). Enter checks all fields and saves the profile; a
field that is not accepted is shown in reverse image, with the
reason on the message line.
:P.
F4 shows the values that can be chosen when the cursor is in
Database type, Status, Encryption or Trace.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/RDB'.
RDB name - Help
:XH4.RDB name
:P.
The name used in CONNECT TO, up to 18 characters. It is also the
name of the RDB directory entry (see option 8). It is typed when the
profile is added and cannot be changed afterwards.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/TEXT'.
Description - Help
:XH4.Description
:P.
A text for the list of your databases. It has no effect.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/TYPE'.
Database type - Help
:XH4.Database type
:P.
PG for PostgreSQL, MYSQL for MySQL and compatible servers, MSSQL for
Microsoft SQL Server.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/STS'.
Status - Help
:XH4.Status
:P.
A is active. D is disabled&colon. CONNECT TO is refused with SQLCODE
-30061 until the profile is set to A again. Use D to close a database
for a while without losing the profile.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/HOST'.
Host - Help
:XH4.Host
:P.
The host name or the IP address of the database server, without
blanks. A long name continues on the second line; fill the first
line before the second.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/PORT'.
Port - Help
:XH4.Port
:P.
The TCP port of the server. 0 means the usual port of the database
type&colon. 5432 for PostgreSQL, 3306 for MySQL, 1433 for SQL Server.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/DBNM'.
Database - Help
:XH4.Database
:P.
The database on the server. Upper and lower case are kept as typed,
and most servers tell them apart.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/USER'.
User - Help
:XH4.User
:P.
The user for the sign-on to the database server. It is a user of
that server, not a user profile of this system. Upper and lower case
are kept as typed. CONNECT TO name USER x USING y replaces the user
and the password of the profile for that connection. The system
hands the driver ten characters of each at most, so a longer user
or password has to be stored in the profile.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/PWD'.
Password - Help
:XH4.Password
:P.
Shows whether a password is stored. F10 sets or removes it; see the
topic about the password.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/TLS'.
Encryption (TLS) - Help
:XH4.Encryption (TLS)
:P.
Whether the connection to the database server is encrypted&colon.
:PARML.
:PT.*OFF
:PD.The connection is not encrypted.
:PT.*PREFER
:PD.Encrypted when the server offers it, otherwise not.
:PT.*REQUIRE
:PD.
Encrypted, or the connection fails. The certificate of the server is
not checked.
:PT.*VERIFY
:PD.
Encrypted, and the certificate of the server is checked. The
connection fails when the certificate is not trusted.
:EPARML.
:P.
An option of the profile can replace this field with the exact mode
of the database client (sslmode, tls, encrypt).
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/TMO'.
Connect timeout - Help
:XH4.Connect timeout
:P.
Seconds to wait for the server when the connection is opened. 0
means 10.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/ALW'.
Allowed users - Help
:XH4.Allowed users
:P.
Who may connect with this profile. *ALL, the value of a new profile,
lets every user of this system connect. Instead of *ALL, up to three
user profiles or group profiles of this system can be named. A job
can then connect only when the user it runs under is one of them, or
has one of them as its group profile or as one of its supplemental
groups. The names must exist when they are typed; upper and lower
case make no difference.
:P.
The driver checks the list on every CONNECT, also when the statement
gives USER and USING. Special authorities do not count&colon. a user
with *ALLOBJ who is not in the list is refused as well, and so is
option 9 of this command when it is run by such a user. A refused
CONNECT ends with SQLCODE -30082 and SQLSTATE 28000, and message
CPF9897 in the job log names the user and the profile.
:P.
The list is kept as option allowed_users of the profile. A list of
more than three names is kept there, with option 7; this display
then shows the first three names and does not change the list.
Whoever may change file XDBPRO of the library may change the list.
The file has public authority *USE&colon. every job can read the
profiles, only the owner of the file and users with *ALLOBJ can
change them.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/CCSID'.
Column CCSID - Help
:XH4.Column CCSID
:P.
The CCSID in which character data is given to Db2. 0 means the CCSID
of the job, which is the usual choice. 65535 is not allowed.
Characters that this CCSID does not have arrive as a substitution
character; no error is reported for them.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/TXTL'.
Text col. length - Help
:XH4.Text col. length
:P.
The length given to character columns for which the server reports
no length, such as text in PostgreSQL or varchar(max) in SQL Server.
0 means 1000; the most is 32000. A longer value is cut at this
length without a warning.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/NSCL'.
Numeric scale - Help
:XH4.Numeric scale
:P.
The number of decimals given to numeric columns for which the server
reports no scale. The most is 20.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/TRC'.
Trace - Help
:XH4.Trace
:P.
Y writes every request of the connections of this profile to stream
file /tmp/xdb_name.log, where name is the RDB name. The file grows
and holds the text of the SQL statements, so set the trace back to N
when the problem is found.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/CHG'.
Last change - Help
:XH4.Last change
:P.
When the profile was last changed and by whom.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/PRF/KEYS'.
Function keys of a profile - Help
:XH4.Function keys
:PARML.
:PT.F1=Help
:PD.
Show the help for the place the cursor is in. F2 in the help shows
the help of the whole display.
:PT.Enter
:PD.Check and save the profile.
:PT.F3=Exit
:PD.Go back to the list without saving.
:PT.F4=Prompt
:PD.Show the values of the field the cursor is in.
:PT.F10=Password
:PD.
Set or remove the password. A new profile must be saved with Enter
first.
:PT.F12=Cancel
:PD.Go back without saving.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ------------------------------------------------------------ password
:HELP NAME='XDB/PASSWORD'.
The password - Help
:XH3.The password
:P.
F10 on a profile opens the password window. Type the new password
and type it again on the repeat line; it is not shown. A password
longer than 60 characters continues on the second line (characters
61 to 120). Upper and lower case are kept as typed. Enter stores it.
F10 in the window removes the stored password. F12 leaves it as it
is.
:P.
The password is stored encrypted in validation list XDBVLDL, which
is created with public authority *EXCLUDE. It is not in a file, is
never shown, and cannot be read with a query. The driver reads it
with the authority of its owner when a
connection is opened and keeps it in the memory of the job while the
connection is open, to open the connection again when the server
has dropped it.
:P.
A profile can do without a stored password&colon.
:UL.
:LI.
The program gives it on the connect&colon. CONNECT TO name USER x
USING y.
:LI.
Option passfile names a stream file with lines of the form
host&colon.port&colon.database&colon.user&colon.password.
:EUL.
:EHELP.
.* ------------------------------------------------------------- options
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPTIONS'.
The options of a profile - Help
:XH3.The options of a profile
:P.
Option 7 on the list shows the options of one profile. An option is
a keyword with a value. Column K tells its kind&colon. C is a keyword
handed to the database client when the connection is opened, D is a
setting of the driver itself. A profile needs no options; add one
only to change a behaviour.
:P.
F6 adds an option. F4 on the keyword shows the keywords that exist
for the database type of the profile, with a description. F4 on the
value shows the values a keyword accepts, when it has a fixed set.
2=Change changes the value, 4=Delete removes the option.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPT/LIST'.
The list of options - Help
:XH4.The list of options
:P.
One line for each option of the profile&colon. the keyword, its kind
in column K and its value. Type 2 next to an option to change its
value, or 4 to delete it after a confirmation, and press Enter. F6
adds an option. A long value is shown cut in the list; option 2
shows it whole.
:P.
The keywords that exist, and what each of them does, are listed
further on in this help (F2).
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPT/KEYS'.
Function keys of the options - Help
:XH4.Function keys
:PARML.
:PT.F1=Help
:PD.
Show the help for the place the cursor is in. F2 in the help shows
the help of the whole display.
:PT.F3=Exit
:PD.Go back to the list of profiles.
:PT.F6=Add
:PD.Add an option to the profile.
:PT.F12=Cancel
:PD.Go back to the list of profiles.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPT/WIN'.
One option - Help
:XH4.Adding or changing an option
:P.
The window shows one option of the profile. When an option is added
(F6) the keyword and the value are typed; when it is changed (option
2) only the value. Description, Values and Kind come from the
dictionary of the keywords and are shown as soon as the keyword is
known. Enter checks the option and saves it. F12 closes the window
and leaves the options as they are.
:P.
A profile has every keyword once. To give a keyword another value,
change the option that is there.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPT/KEY'.
Keyword - Help
:XH4.Keyword
:P.
The name of the option, in lower case. Only keywords of the
dictionary are accepted, and only those that exist for the database
type of the profile. F4 with the cursor in this field shows them
with their descriptions; type 1 next to one to take it. The keyword
of an option that is there cannot be changed&colon. delete the
option and add another.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPT/VAL'.
Value - Help
:XH4.Value
:P.
The value of the option, up to 200 characters on four lines; fill a
line before the next one. Upper and lower case are kept as typed.
When the line Values shows a list of values, only one of them is
accepted, and F4 with the cursor in the value shows the list to
choose from. When it shows (any value), the keyword takes any value;
the driver or the database client checks it when a connection is
opened.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPT/ALL'.
Keywords for every type - Help
:XH4.Keywords for every database type
:PARML.
:PT.idle_check
:PD.
Seconds a connection may be idle before the driver tests it at the
next statement. Without the option it is 2. When the test finds the
connection gone, the driver opens it again; the program notices
nothing unless a unit of work was open, in which case the statement
fails with SQLCODE -911 and the unit of work is lost. 0 never tests.
:PT.ping_timeout
:PD.
Seconds to wait for the answer to that test. Without the option it
is 5.
:PT.joblog
:PD.
Which errors of the database server are written to the job log as
message CPF9897&colon. long (only texts too long for the SQL message,
the default), all, or off.
:PT.trim_char
:PD.
on (the default) removes the trailing blanks of fixed-length
character host variables before they are sent. off sends them as
they are.
:PT.user_case
:PD.
The user given on CONNECT USER arrives in upper case from Db2. lower
(the default) turns it to lower case, asis leaves it.
:PT.product
:PD.
The product identifier reported to Db2, 8 characters. Leave it out
unless a tool needs a particular one.
:PT.passfile
:PD.
A stream file with passwords, used when none is stored; see the
topic about the password.
:PT.allowed_users
:PD.
The user profiles and group profiles that may connect with the
profile, separated by blanks, or *ALL. It is the field Allowed users
of the profile, whose help tells how the check works; use the
option when more than three names are needed. Without the option
every user may connect.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPT/PG'.
Keywords for PostgreSQL - Help
:XH4.Keywords for PostgreSQL (PG)
:PARML.
:PT.savepoints
:PD.
on (the default) sets a savepoint before every statement of a unit
of work, so that a failing statement does not make PostgreSQL refuse
all the following ones until the rollback. off leaves that out.
:PT.long_values
:PD.
cut (the default) asks the server for no more than the text column
length of long columns. full reads them whole; the driver still
gives Db2 no more than the text column length.
:PT.lob_direct
:PD.
For the file functions (GET_BLOB_FROM_FILE and the others)&colon.
the largest value, in KB, that travels in one piece. The default
is 4096, the limit 6144. A larger value is staged in a large object
of the server and moved in pieces. 0 stages every value.
:PT.sslmode
:PD.
The exact sslmode of the PostgreSQL client&colon. disable, allow,
prefer, require, verify-ca or verify-full. It replaces the
Encryption field.
:PT.sslrootcert
:PD.
A stream file with the certificates of the certificate authorities
that are trusted.
:PT.application_name
:PD.The name shown for the connection in pg_stat_activity.
:PT.options
:PD.
Options for the server session, for example -c search_path=sales.
:PT.keepalives_idle
:PD.Seconds without traffic before TCP keepalive starts.
:PT.target_session_attrs
:PD.
The kind of server wanted when the host is one of several&colon.
any, read-write, read-only, primary or standby.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPT/MYSQL'.
Keywords for MySQL - Help
:XH4.Keywords for MySQL (MYSQL)
:PARML.
:PT.init_sql
:PD.
A statement run right after the connection is opened, for example
to set the time zone or the SQL mode of the session.
:PT.tls
:PD.
The exact TLS mode&colon. off, prefer, require or verify. It
replaces the Encryption field.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/OPT/MSSQL'.
Keywords for SQL Server - Help
:XH4.Keywords for Microsoft SQL Server (MSSQL)
:PARML.
:PT.init_sql
:PD.A statement run right after the connection is opened.
:PT.encrypt
:PD.
The exact encryption mode&colon. off, prefer, on or strict. It
replaces the Encryption field.
:PT.tls_verify
:PD.on checks the certificate of the server, off does not.
:PT.tds
:PD.
7.1 forces the old protocol version, which SQL Server 2000 needs.
:PT.charset
:PD.
The CCSID of varchar data, when the driver cannot tell it from the
collation of the server.
:PT.string_params
:PD.
The type in which character host variables are sent&colon. auto,
varchar or nvarchar. varchar lets SQL Server use an index on a
varchar column.
:PT.datetime2
:PD.
on sends dates and times as date, time and datetime2. off sends
them as datetime, for servers older than SQL Server 2008.
:PT.appname
:PD.The application name shown on the server.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
.* ------------------------------------------------------------- windows
:HELP NAME='XDB/PROMPT'.
Choosing a value - Help
:XH4.Choosing a value (F4)
:P.
The window lists the values that can be put into the field the
cursor was in. Type 1 next to one of them and press Enter; the value
is put into the field. F12 closes the window and leaves the field as
it is. Page down shows more values when the list ends with More.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/CONFIRM'.
Confirmation - Help
:XH4.Confirmation
:P.
The window says what is about to be done&colon. deleting a profile
with its options and its stored password, deleting an option, or
adding an RDB directory entry. Enter does it. F12 closes the window
and nothing is done.
:EHELP.
.* ----------------------------------------------------------- RDB entry
:HELP NAME='XDB/RDBENTRY'.
The RDB directory entry - Help
:XH3.The RDB directory entry
:P.
Db2 finds a remote database through the RDB directory. An entry
with the RDB name of the profile and the driver program XDBARD as its
program makes CONNECT TO that name call the driver. Without the entry the
profile cannot be used.
:P.
Option 8 on the list adds it after a confirmation. It runs
:XMP.
ADDRDBDIRE RDB(name) RMTLOCNAME(*ARDPGM) ARDPGM(library/XDBARD)
  TEXT('description')
:EXMP.
:P.
with the library the driver is in. The text of the entry, which the
list of WRKRDBDIRE shows, is the description of the profile; for a
profile without a description it is the database type and the host.
The entry is for the whole system. When it exists already, the
option says so and changes nothing, its text included&colon. a
description changed later is brought there with CHGRDBDIRE RDB(name)
TEXT('...'). Deleting a profile does not remove the entry; use the
Work with RDB Directory Entries (WRKRDBDIRE) command for that.
:EHELP.
.* ---------------------------------------------------------------- test
:HELP NAME='XDB/TEST'.
The connection test - Help
:XH3.The connection test
:P.
Option 9 on the list connects to the database exactly as a program
would, through CONNECT TO, and shows the result in a window&colon.
the version of the server when the connection was opened, or the
SQLCODE, the SQLSTATE and the message of the server when it was not.
The connection is closed again. Nothing is changed on the server.
:P.
The test needs the RDB directory entry (option 8) and uses the
stored password.
:EHELP.
.* ----------------------------------------------------------------- SQL
:HELP NAME='XDB/SQL'.
Using a profile - Help
:XH3.Using a profile from SQL and from a program
:P.
:HP2.Interactive SQL (STRSQL)&colon.:EHP2.
:XMP.
CONNECT TO name
SELECT ... FROM schema.table
COMMIT
DISCONNECT name
CONNECT RESET
:EXMP.
:P.
The statements are written in the SQL of the server, with its names
and its functions. A name with lower case letters is put between
double quotation marks where the server asks for that.
:P.
:HP2.Connections&colon.:EHP2.
:UL.
:LI.
A job can have several connections at the same time. SET CONNECTION
name makes another one the current connection; open cursors of the
one left behind stay open.
:LI.
A connection stays open until DISCONNECT, until RELEASE followed by
COMMIT, or until the job or the activation group of the program
ends.
:LI.
The connections run under commitment control. The first statement,
a SELECT too, opens a unit of work on the server. DISCONNECT is
refused with message SQL0428 while it is open&colon. run COMMIT or
ROLLBACK first. Commit also before a session is left idle, because
the open unit of work can hold locks on the server.
:LI.
COMMIT and ROLLBACK apply to all the connections of the unit of
work, the local database included. A commit that cannot take place
on a server fails as a whole&colon. see COMMIT and ROLLBACK below.
:LI.
CONNECT TO name USER x USING y signs on to the server with that
user and password instead of those of the profile. Ten characters
of each reach the driver; what is longer is cut without a message.
The user arrives in upper case and is turned to lower case unless
option user_case says asis. A profile that names its allowed users
is checked in the same way with and without USER.
:EUL.
:P.
:HP2.COMMIT and ROLLBACK&colon.:EHP2.
:P.
The driver takes part in the commit of the system, so that a commit
which cannot take place on a server fails for the program.
:UL.
:LI.
The first server connection decides the commit. The system prepares
everything else, then the driver commits on that server; when that
fails, the system rolls back everything else, the local database
included. The local database and this one server are committed
together or not at all.
:LI.
Every further server connection is asked, before anything is
committed, whether its server is still there, and is committed after
the first. A server that is gone makes the whole unit of work roll
back.
:LI.
A connection that only read never makes a COMMIT fail by being
lost.
:EUL.
:P.
When the commit fails, COMMIT returns SQLCODE -175, SQLSTATE 58028,
and the unit of work is rolled back everywhere. The job log has the
reason from the driver in message CPF9897 (it starts with XDB and
the name of the connection), followed by CPD83EC, CPD83D7, CPF83DB
and SQL0175. SELECT * FROM XDB_LAST_ERROR shows the reason too. Run
ROLLBACK, then run the unit of work again or stop.
:P.
The reasons&colon.
:PARML.
:PT.the connection to the server was lost before it
:PD.
The session ended since the last statement&colon. the server closed
an idle session, a firewall dropped the connection, the server was
restarted. What the unit of work changed on the server went with it.
:PT.what this unit of work changed on the server was lost earlier
:PD.
A statement of this unit of work was already answered with SQLCODE
-911. A unit of work that got -911 cannot be committed, whatever
the program did after it.
:PT.COMMIT refused by the server
:PD.
The server was asked and said no&colon. a constraint that is checked
at the end, a conflict with another transaction, or on PostgreSQL
with option savepoints off a statement that had failed.
:PT.the server ended the session instead of committing
:PD.
The server answered the COMMIT with its last message and closed the
session.
:PT.whether it has committed is not known
:PD.
The connection broke while the server worked on the COMMIT. The
rest is rolled back, but the server may have committed&colon. look at
the data on the server.
:EPARML.
:P.
A unit of work only reads on a connection when it runs nothing there
but queries and SET or USE statements. A query counts as a change
when its text has one of the words INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE, MERGE,
INTO, CALL, EXEC, EXECUTE, NEXTVAL, SETVAL, CREATE, DROP, ALTER or
TRUNCATE. A function that changes data and is called in a plain
SELECT is not seen.
:P.
Not covered&colon. the second and further server connections are only
asked whether their server is still there. A server that is there
and then refuses its own commit (a constraint that is checked at the
end, a conflict with another transaction), or that fails in the
moment between the question and its commit, loses its changes while
the rest is committed; message CPF9897 in the job log says so (THE
REST OF THE UNIT OF WORK IS COMMITTED). Connect first to the server
whose changes matter most. The servers are not asked for a real
two-phase commit.
:P.
Program XDBLOST in the sources (test/xdblost.sqlrpgle) lets sessions
end inside units of work and prints what COMMIT answers.
:P.
:HP2.Programs with embedded SQL&colon.:EHP2.
:UL.
:LI.
Create the program with RDB(*LOCAL) and COMMIT(*CHG) on the
CRTSQLRPGI command. The name of a relational database in place of
*LOCAL works too, but is not needed&colon. no SQL package has to be
made, and one program can connect to every profile. A program
created with RDB(*NONE) cannot connect (SQL0862); on some systems
that is what the command does when RDB is left out. COMMIT(*NONE)
is not accepted (SQL5028).
:LI.
Use dynamic SQL&colon. PREPARE and EXECUTE, and for a query a cursor
declared for a prepared statement, with parameter markers for the
values.
:LI.
For a local table that is not journaled, add WITH NC to the
statement, or use the record operations of the language, which do
not depend on the current connection.
:LI.
To read many rows, fetch them in blocks&colon. FETCH cursor FOR n
ROWS INTO a data structure array. Take the number of rows from
SQLERRD(3); it can be less than n before the end. The driver brings
up to 200 rows from the server with each exchange whatever n is,
so a few hundred is enough.
:LI.
One program can read a server and fill a local table. With the
record operations of the language on the local side the connection
is never changed. With SQL on the local side change the connection
once for each block of rows, not for each row, and write the block
with one INSERT (INSERT INTO table n ROWS VALUES(array)). Commit
at the end&colon. COMMIT closes the cursor on the server too.
Program XDBETL in the sources (test/xdbetl.sqlrpgle) is a sample
with three servers and the local database in one program.
:EUL.
:P.
:HP2.Long values and large objects&colon.:EHP2.
:UL.
:LI.
The interface of the system that the driver is called through does
not carry large objects (BLOB, CLOB, DBCLOB). A column without a
length limit, such as text, json or bytea in PostgreSQL or
varchar(max) in SQL Server, arrives as a character value of the
Text col. length of the profile, 32000 at most. What is longer is
cut without a warning.
:LI.
Such a value can be fetched into a CLOB variable or into a CLOB file
reference variable (SQLTYPE(CLOB_FILE) in RPG)&colon. Db2 writes the
stream file. It holds the value as it arrived, cut at the same
length.
:LI.
A bytea column of PostgreSQL arrives as text&colon. \x and two
hexadecimal digits for every byte. It cannot be fetched into a BLOB
variable or a BLOB file (SQL0303).
:LI.
A large object variable or a file reference variable cannot be the
value of a parameter marker. With a file reference the statement
fails with SQLCODE -804; message CPF9897 in the job log and
XDB_LAST_ERROR tell which host variable it was. A CLOB or BLOB
variable is refused by Db2 with SQL0311 before the driver is
called. Pass the value in a character variable (VARCHAR), or use
the file functions that follow.
:EUL.
:P.
:HP2.Large values from and to stream files&colon.:EHP2.
A value of megabytes cannot pass through a host variable, but it
can pass beside Db2&colon. the statement names a stream file, and
the driver moves the value between that file and the server, on the
connection and in the unit of work of the statement. Four functions
in the text of a statement ask for it, on PostgreSQL, MySQL and SQL
Server connections alike. The examples are written for PostgreSQL;
the rest of a statement is in the language of its server, so the
name of a file is put together with CONCAT(...) on MySQL and with +
on SQL Server.
:XMP.
INSERT INTO DOC (ID, BODY) VALUES(?, GET_BLOB_FROM_FILE(?))
UPDATE DOC SET NOTE = GET_CLOB_FROM_FILE('/home/me/n.txt')
  WHERE ID = 7
SELECT ID, PUT_BLOB_TO_FILE(BODY, '/home/me/d' || ID || '.pdf')
  FROM DOC WHERE YEAR = 2026
SELECT PUT_CLOB_TO_FILE(NOTE, ?, 16, 1252) FROM DOC WHERE ID = ?
:EXMP.
:PARML.
:PT.GET_BLOB_FROM_FILE(name), GET_CLOB_FROM_FILE(name)
:PD.
The bytes, or the text, of a stream file as a value, in a statement
that returns no rows (INSERT, UPDATE, CALL). The names and the
meaning are those of the functions of Db2 for i. name is a string
or a parameter marker. Text is converted from the CCSID of the
file.
:PT.PUT_BLOB_TO_FILE(value, name, option)
:PD.
A column of the result of a query. Every row that is fetched writes
its value to the file named by the second argument, which can be
any expression of the server, also one built from other columns.
The column itself is the number of bytes the file grew by, or null
when the value is null; then no file is touched. option is
optional and has the values of a file reference variable&colon. 8
the file must not exist, 16 create or replace (the default), 32
create or append. A new file has CCSID 65535.
:PT.PUT_CLOB_TO_FILE(value, name, option, ccsid)
:PD.
The same for text. ccsid is optional and is the CCSID a new file
gets; the default is 1208 (UTF-8), which loses no character. A
file that exists keeps the CCSID it has&colon. text that replaces
its content or is appended to it is converted to that CCSID.
:EPARML.
:UL.
:LI.
The functions are found in the statement text, so the statement
must be dynamic&colon. PREPARE and EXECUTE, or a cursor on a
prepared statement. In interactive SQL they can be typed as they
are. PUT_BLOB_TO_FILE and PUT_CLOB_TO_FILE must be columns of the
outermost SELECT, with no * in front of them.
:LI.
The files are opened as the user of the job, with the authority of
that user. Others can read a new file, the owner can change it.
The name of a file to write comes from the server with the row&colon.
build it from values you trust.
:LI.
The job never holds more than a piece of a value, whatever its
size. The limit is the one of the server.
:LI.
PostgreSQL&colon. a value up to option lob_direct (4 MB) travels in
one piece. A larger one passes through a large object of the
server, which the driver writes or reads in pieces of 1 MB and
removes again in the same unit of work. The limit is 1 GB. On a
server that only allows reading, values up to lob_direct can be
written to files, larger ones not.
:LI.
MySQL and MariaDB&colon. a file is sent as the value of a parameter
in pieces of 1 MB; a value for a file is written while its row
arrives. The limit is max_allowed_packet of the server (64 MB by
default on MySQL 8, 16 MB on MariaDB). A longer file is refused; a
longer value to write ends the OPEN with SQLSTATE 54000.
:LI.
SQL Server 2005 and later&colon. a file is sent as a varbinary(max)
or nvarchar(max) parameter in pieces; a value for a file is written
while its row arrives. The limit is 2 GB.
:LI.
SQL Server 2000&colon. a file is sent as an image or ntext
parameter, which is good for the columns of INSERT and UPDATE.
PUT_BLOB_TO_FILE and PUT_CLOB_TO_FILE are not available there
(SQLCODE -84).
:LI.
On MySQL and SQL Server a file is written by the fetch that reads
its row, one row at a time. A file that was written to its end
stays, also when another column of the same row then fails.
:LI.
PUT_BLOB_TO_FILE and PUT_CLOB_TO_FILE cannot be combined with UNION,
EXCEPT or INTERSECT.
:LI.
Text read from a file must be valid in its CCSID. Text that is not
is refused with SQLSTATE 22021 and the place of the first wrong
byte.
:LI.
When a transfer fails, no half file is left&colon. a new or
replaced file is removed, a file that was extended is cut back. A
file that was written stays when the unit of work is rolled back.
:LI.
An error with a file is SQLCODE -452 (SQLSTATE 428A1) with the
reason codes of message SQL0452&colon. 1 the name is not valid or
names a directory, 2 the name is too long, 4 the file or its
directory is not found, 5 the file exists and option 8 was given.
No authority is SQLCODE -551, a file in use -913. Message CPF9897 in
the job log names the file.
:EUL.
:P.
:HP2.Stored procedures of the server&colon.:EHP2.
A procedure of the server is called with the statement of the
server, as dynamic SQL&colon. CALL name(...) on PostgreSQL and
MySQL, EXEC name ... on SQL Server. A program prepares and executes
it; in STRSQL it is typed. The rows a procedure returns are fetched
through a cursor on its call&colon.
:XMP.
PREPARE S1 FROM 'CALL MYPROC(?)'       (SQL Server: 'EXEC MYPROC ?')
DECLARE C1 CURSOR FOR S1
OPEN C1 USING :N
FETCH C1 INTO :A, :B
:EXMP.
:UL.
:LI.
Values go in as parameter markers. An EXECUTE hands nothing back,
no output parameter and no return code&colon. a value that comes
back is left with the server and read with a query. On MySQL a
variable of the session takes it&colon. CALL P(?, ?, @TOTAL) and
then SELECT @TOTAL. On PostgreSQL write a function and select from
it. On SQL Server put the value into a temporary table in the same
batch as the EXEC.
:LI.
The procedure runs when the cursor is opened, once for each OPEN,
with the values of that OPEN. The cursor has its first result set;
further result sets are read and dropped.
:LI.
All rows are read at the OPEN and kept in the memory of the job
until they are fetched. For very many rows let the procedure fill a
temporary table and read that with an ordinary cursor.
:LI.
MySQL tells the columns of the rows only when the procedure has
run. Db2 gets them with the OPEN, so FETCH INTO works as with any
cursor; before its first OPEN the statement has no columns to
describe. EXPLAIN and some SHOW statements of MySQL are opened and
fetched in the same way. SQL Server describes a procedure from its
text, without running it; where that is not possible the columns
come with the OPEN too.
:LI.
A SQL Server procedure with more than one way through it can return
a first result set with another number of columns than it was
described with. The OPEN then gets SQLSTATE 07008 (SQLCODE -438),
and the procedure has run.
:LI.
On PostgreSQL procedures return no rows, and the server refuses a
cursor on a CALL (SQLCODE -104). A function that returns a table is
a query&colon. SELECT * FROM F(?).
:LI.
A procedure that returns no result set has nothing to fetch&colon.
the OPEN gets SQLCODE -517 (SQLSTATE 07005), and the procedure has
run all the same. A procedure that fails gives its error to the
OPEN.
:LI.
A call that is executed and not opened runs the procedure, and the
rows it returns are dropped. STRSQL shows no rows for a CALL&colon.
to look at them there, let the procedure fill a temporary table and
select from it.
:LI.
PUT_BLOB_TO_FILE and PUT_CLOB_TO_FILE cannot be used on the rows of
a call. GET_BLOB_FROM_FILE and GET_CLOB_FROM_FILE can be arguments
of a call.
:LI.
A call counts as a change of the unit of work&colon. what the
procedure did is committed or rolled back with the rest. A
procedure that commits or rolls back by itself is better avoided.
:LI.
Program XDBPROC in the sources (test/xdbproc.sqlrpgle) is a sample
for a MySQL server.
:EUL.
:P.
:HP2.Errors&colon.:EHP2.
For the usual errors, such as a name that is not found, a duplicate
key or a syntax error, Db2 shows its own message. For the others it
shows the class of the error with SQLCODE and SQLSTATE. The complete
message of the server is in the job log when it is long, and
:XMP.
SELECT * FROM XDB_LAST_ERROR
:EXMP.
:P.
returns the last error of the job&colon. SQLCODE, the SQLSTATE of the
server, its error number, the database type, the RDB name and the
whole message. In a program, GET DIAGNOSTICS CONDITION 1 gives the
message text.
:P.
:HP2.What a connection is&colon.:EHP2.
:XMP.
SELECT * FROM XDB_CONNECTION_INFO
:EXMP.
:P.
is answered by the driver itself, on any connection, without asking
the server and without touching the unit of work. It returns rows
of three columns, AREA, NAME and VALUE, with what the driver knows
about the connection. It is there for finding the cause of a
problem, and for a program that wants to know what kind of server
it talks to. In a program the statement can also be written XDB
CONNECTION INFO; prepare it and fetch its rows through a cursor.
Only these two spellings are answered&colon. with a WHERE clause or
a list of columns the statement goes to the server, which does not
know the name.
:P.
The areas and the main rows of each&colon.
:PARML.
:PT.connection
:PD.
rdb, database_type (PostgreSQL, MySQL or SQL Server), host, port,
database, user, profile (where it is kept), connected (when).
:PT.link
:PD.
The connection to the server&colon. protocol, encryption (none, or
the TLS level and the cipher), session_id (the number of the session
on the server&colon. the process on PostgreSQL, the connection id on
MySQL, the SPID on SQL Server), state.
:PT.server
:PD.
product, version as the server reports it, version_number (major
times 100 plus minor, on every server), and what else the server
said when the connection was opened, such as its encoding, its time
zone or the database of the session.
:PT.session
:PD.
commit_mode, unit_of_work (none, open, open with changes, or that
its changes are lost; while it is open DISCONNECT is refused),
commit_resource (the part of the connection in a commit&colon. last
agent, asked before the commit, or only told),
commit_resource_refused (what the system did not allow, with its
message), statements, open_cursors, seconds_since_last_statement.
:PT.counters
:PD.
Since CONNECT&colon. reconnects (how often the driver opened the
connection again after it was lost), last_reconnect,
units_of_work_lost (how often that happened with a unit of work
open), idle_checks, statements_prepared, statements_executed,
rows_changed, cursors_opened, fetch_requests, rows_fetched,
fetches_from_server, commits, commits_failed, rollbacks, errors.
:PT.options
:PD.
The settings in effect&colon. text_len, numeric_scale, ccsid,
trim_char, idle_check, ping_timeout, joblog, trace, and those only
the database type has.
:PT.limits
:PD.
Statements for a connection, parameters for a statement, columns
of a result, connections for a job, rows for each exchange with the
server.
:PT.driver
:PD.
name, version (of the package the objects came from), built (when
the code this job runs was compiled), library, job_ccsid,
connections_in_job.
:EPARML.
:P.
The rows are for people and for diagnosis, and they can change with
a new version of the driver. A program takes the rows it needs by
their names and goes on when one is missing. The row built tells
whether a job still runs the code from before an update&colon. a job
keeps the driver it has loaded until it ends. Program XDBINFO in
the sources (test/xdbinfo.sqlrpgle) prints the rows and shows how a
program picks single values.
:EHELP.
.* --------------------------------------------------------------- setup
:HELP NAME='XDB/SETUP'.
Setup - Help
:XH3.Setup&colon. installing, moving and checking
:P.
XDBARDDRV works from any library of the system ASP. The library is not
asked for&colon. it is the one the program was started from, shown at
the top of the display. The Setup display looks at that library and
lists, one line for each check, what is in order and what is still
to do. Run it after the objects were restored from a package, after
the library was renamed or its objects were moved, after a new
version was restored over an old one, or whenever you want to know
that everything is as it should be.
:P.
:HP2.Nothing is changed unseen&colon.:EHP2.
What a line needs is a small number of CL commands. A window shows
them, exactly as they will run, before anything happens, and they
run only when you press Enter there. Every command is also written
to the job log before it runs. When a command fails, the window says
which one and shows the message; the commands after it are not run.
Setup changes nothing outside the library except the owner profile,
which it creates when you confirm that line. It never changes a
system value, and it never touches profiles or passwords that are
already there.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/OWN'.
Owner profile - Help
:XH4.Owner profile (the field at the top)
:P.
The user profile that is to own the driver and the password list.
Setup proposes XDBOWNER, or the profile that owns the driver already
when the driver runs with the authority of its owner. Type another
name and press Enter to have the lines checked for that profile.
The line Owner profile of the list tells what the profile is for
and how it is created.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/OPT'.
Options of the Setup display - Help
:XH4.Options of the Setup display
:P.
Type an option next to a line and press Enter.
:PARML.
:PT.1=Do it
:PD.
Show the commands of the line and, after Enter, run them and show
the result. F12 in the window leaves the line as it is.
:PT.5=Show the commands
:PD.
Show the commands of the line without running anything.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/LINES'.
The lines of the setup - Help
:XH4.The lines of the Setup display
:P.
Every line is one check of the installation in this library. The
lines are done from top to bottom, because a line can need the
one before it.
:PARML.
:PT.Library is in the system ASP
:PD.
Db2 calls the driver inside every job that connects. A library of an
independent ASP is not there in jobs without that ASP group, so the
objects must be in the system ASP or a basic user ASP. QTEMP cannot
be used either.
:PT.Your special authorities
:PD.
Tells whether you have *SECADM and *ALLOBJ, which the lines about
owners need. Without them, option 5 on those lines shows the
commands for a security officer to run.
:PT.System value QRETSVRSEC
:PD.
The system gives stored passwords back only when this value is 1.
With 0 no password can be stored in a profile; the databases can
still be used with a password given on the connect or with a
password file. The line shows the command; setup does not run it.
:PT.Option dictionary XDBKWD
:PD.The file with the option keywords, which comes with the package.
:PT.Profile files XDBPRF and XDBPRO
:PD.
The files of the profiles and their options. They are never part of
a package, so restoring a package cannot overwrite profiles. Setup
creates them empty from the model files XDBPRFMDL and XDBPROMDL.
:PT.Password list XDBVLDL
:PD.
The validation list with the encrypted passwords. It is never part
of a package. Setup creates it with public authority *EXCLUDE.
:PT.Driver bound to this library
:PD.
In a package the programs of the driver find their service programs
through the library list. Setup binds them to the service programs
of this library (UPDPGM and UPDSRVPGM with SRVPGMLIB), so that the
driver works in every job whatever its library list is. A job that
is connected at that moment keeps the objects it has until it ends.
:PT.Command WRKXDBPRF
:PD.
The command and its help are pointed to the program and the panel
group of this library (CHGCMD), so that they work without the
library list.
:PT.Objects have an owner of this system
:PD.
A restore gives an object to profile QDFTOWN, the default owner,
when the profile that owned it where it was saved does not exist on
this system. That is so with a package that was built by a profile
of its own. The line gives every object of the package that is
found with QDFTOWN as its owner to QPGMR, one CHGOBJOWN for each
object, which needs the authority to change owners (in practice
*ALLOBJ). The objects work in the meantime. The driver XDB is not
among them&colon. it gets its owner in the last line.
:PT.Owner profile
:PD.
The driver has to read the passwords for users who must not be able
to read them themselves. For that it runs with the authority of the
profile that owns it. Setup proposes profile XDBOWNER; another name
can be typed at the top of the display. The profile is created with
no password, so that nobody can sign on with it, with no special
authority, and it owns nothing but the driver and the password
list.
:PT.Driver and passwords owned by it
:PD.
Service program XDB and validation list XDBVLDL are given to the
owner profile, the public gets no authority to the list, and the
service program is set to adopt the authority of its owner
(CHGOBJOWN, GRTOBJAUT, CHGSRVPGM USRPRF(*OWNER)). A package comes
without adoption; it is switched on here, where you see it.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/STS'.
Status of a line - Help
:XH4.Status of a line
:PARML.
:PT.OK
:PD.The check found everything in order.
:PT.To do
:PD.Setup can do it&colon. use option 1 on the line, or F10.
:PT.A remark
:PD.
Something to know or to hand to somebody else, such as a system
value. Option 5 shows the command when there is one.
:PT.Failing
:PD.
The installation cannot work like this and setup cannot repair it,
for example when the library is in an independent ASP or objects of
the package are missing.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/KEYS'.
Function keys of the setup - Help
:XH4.Function keys
:PARML.
:PT.F1=Help
:PD.
Show the help for the place the cursor is in. F2 in the help shows
the help of the whole display.
:PT.F3=Exit
:PD.Leave the Setup display.
:PT.F5=Check again
:PD.Run all the checks again.
:PT.F10=Do all that is to do
:PD.
Go through the lines that are to do, from top to bottom, each with
its window and its confirmation. F12 skips a line, F3 stops.
:PT.F12=Cancel
:PD.Leave the Setup display.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/CMD'.
The commands of a line - Help
:XH4.The window with the commands of a line
:P.
The window shows the CL commands a line of the Setup display needs,
exactly as they will run, with a note above them that tells what
they are for. With option 1 and with F10, Enter runs the commands
and a second window shows for each of them whether it was done; the
first command that fails stops the line, and its message is shown.
F12 leaves the line as it is. With option 5 nothing is run and Enter
only closes the window.
:P.
A line with more commands than the window holds is shown in pages.
The bottom line then says that more follows, and Enter shows the
next page; the commands are run only after Enter on the last page,
where the bottom line says so. F12 on any page leaves the line as it
is.
:P.
A command that setup never runs itself, such as the change of a
system value, is only shown, for the person who may run it.
:EHELP.
:HELP NAME='XDB/SETUP/HOW'.
Installing and removing - Help
:XH4.Installing, updating, passwords afterwards, removing
:P.
:HP2.Installing from a package&colon.:EHP2.
:OL.
:LI.
Create the library, for example XDB, and restore the objects of the
package into it (RSTOBJ with RSTLIB).
:LI.
Add the library to the library list (ADDLIBLE) and run WRKXDBPRF.
Until the command has been set up it finds its program through the
library list.
:LI.
The Setup display comes by itself. Press F10 and confirm the lines.
:LI.
F3 leads to the list of profiles. Add the profiles, set their
passwords, test them with option 9 and add the RDB directory entries
with option 8.
:EOL.
:P.
:HP2.Updating to a newer package&colon.:EHP2.
Restore the newer objects over the same library, with one parameter
more than for the first restore&colon.
:XMP.
RSTOBJ OBJ(*ALL) SAVLIB(CESCO2) DEV(*SAVF) SAVF(...)
  RSTLIB(library) ALWOBJDIF(*OWNER *FILELVL)
:EXMP.
:P.
The parameter is needed because objects of the library have another
owner than those of the package (the driver belongs to the owner
profile since the setup, and the others can differ too), and because
the option dictionary and the two model files are made anew for
every package. Without it the restore leaves those objects out, and
old objects stay beside new ones; repeat the restore with the
parameter then. It asks for special authority *ALLOBJ. The messages
CPF370B, one for each object whose owner differs, and CPF3848 are
expected.
:P.
Then start the program of the library (CALL library/XDBPRFM), press
F9 and F10&colon. the lines about the binding, the command and the
owner are to do again, and connections may fail until they are done.
The profiles, their options and their passwords are kept.
:P.
:HP2.Who can store passwords afterwards&colon.:EHP2.
The password list belongs to the owner profile and the public has
no authority to it. A person who keeps the profiles needs *CHANGE
authority to XDBVLDL, or *ALLOBJ, to store or remove a password.
:P.
:HP2.Removing XDBARDDRV&colon.:EHP2.
Remove the RDB directory entries of the profiles (WRKRDBDIRE),
delete the library, and delete the owner profile.
:EHELP.
.* ------------------------------------------------------------ problems
:HELP NAME='XDB/PROBLEMS'.
When something does not work - Help
:XH3.When something does not work
:PARML.
:PT.CONNECT fails
:PD.
Run option 9 on the profile; its window shows the answer of the
server. SQLCODE -30061 means that the profile is disabled or
missing, or that the server does not have the database. -30080
means that the server was not reached (host, port, firewall, TLS).
-30082 means that the server refused the user or the password, that
the stored password could not be read, or that the user of the job
is not among the allowed users of the profile. The reason is in the
job log, in message CPF9897, unless option joblog of the profile is
off.
:PT.CONNECT is refused for some users only
:PD.
Message CPF9897 in the job log says that the user is not among the
users allowed for the profile. Look at Allowed users of the profile
(option 2), or at option allowed_users (option 7)&colon. the user of
the job has to be named there, or be a member of a group profile
named there.
:PT.A large object cannot be read or written
:PD.
Large object variables do not work with these connections. Use the
file functions (GET_BLOB_FROM_FILE, PUT_BLOB_TO_FILE and the two
for text)&colon. see the parts about long values and about large
values from and to stream files in the topic about using a profile
from SQL.
:PT.SQLCODE -452 with a file function
:PD.
The file could not be read or written. Message CPF9897 in the job
log names the file and the reason; SELECT * FROM XDB_LAST_ERROR
shows the same text. A name without a directory is looked for in
the current directory of the job.
:PT.A local program cannot connect (SQL0862)
:PD.
The program was created with RDB(*NONE). Create it again with
RDB(*LOCAL) COMMIT(*CHG).
:PT.Relational database not in the directory
:PD.
The RDB directory entry is missing; use option 8.
:PT.Characters arrive wrong
:PD.
Check Column CCSID. A character the CCSID does not have cannot be
shown. Line ends and tabs inside a text are kept as control
characters, which a 5250 display cannot show.
:PT.A text is cut
:PD.
Raise Text col. length, up to 32000.
:PT.A statement fails after a pause
:PD.
The server dropped the idle connection while a unit of work was
open (SQLCODE -911). Run ROLLBACK and start the unit of work again;
a COMMIT would be refused. Commit before long pauses.
:PT.COMMIT fails with SQLCODE -175
:PD.
The commit could not take place on a server and the whole unit of
work was rolled back. Message CPF9897 in the job log, just before
CPD83EC, tells which connection and why; SELECT * FROM
XDB_LAST_ERROR shows the same. See COMMIT and ROLLBACK in the topic
about using a profile from SQL.
:PT.The job log ends with MCH3601, CPF8367 and CPF9999
:PD.
A job that has used the local database and a connection through
the driver gets these messages, with CPF4404 and CPF93B7, once when
it ends. They come from the system after everything was committed
or rolled back, and the job ends normally. Nothing has to be done;
see Limits in the README.
:PT.The list says the setup is not complete
:PD.
Press F9. The lines that are not OK tell what is missing; see the
topic about the setup.
:PT.Connections fail in some jobs only
:PD.
The driver may still be bound through the library list, which those
jobs do not have. Press F9 and look at the line about the binding.
:PT.Nothing explains it
:PD.
Set Trace to Y, repeat the failing step, and read
/tmp/xdb_name.log. Set Trace back to N afterwards.
:EPARML.
:EHELP.
